A track button pressed while a queued pattern change is in flight (about
250 ms around the change, a chain or a single switch) halted at
gk_stock_part_refresh_writer_report_fatal_part_refresh_fatal_context:
gk_stock_current_context_validate returns GK_ERR_BUSY while
__gk_sequencer_latch_queue_handoff is set, and the Part-refresh writer
wrapper took every negative return as the context fatal (measured under
the ColdFire port, 5 Oct 2026, Octakit alone and bottleservice; Em's
ems-octakit#5 setting; a Discord user's bottleservice image 88 halted in
the same handoff at gk_pattern_queue_request_ipl_fatal).

Her sibling wrappers (machine_selection.S, midi_bit_toggle.S) test BUSY
before the fatal and return it. This writer wraps a stock call with no
caller to return to, so on BUSY it runs the stock writer the way her
QUIESCED path does, with no Kit lease: the writer's four validator calls
and the marker's compare become same-length jsr swaps into
octabam_part_refresh_busy.S (her layout and every stock slice offset
kept; the unit is listed last). d7 carries a third token state through
the stock body, UNWRAPPED ('FU' beside her ARMED 'FR' and WRITTEN 'FW';
stock's writer saves and uses d2-d6/a2-a5 only), and the dirty-store
marker replays stock's store for it. A BUSY from the post-write
validation (a handoff that began during a wrapped write) releases the
descriptor and returns. What the Kit workspace misses by not seeing an
unwrapped run's dirty marks is not measured.

